1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,318
Turmeric, cumin, mustard
seeds, red chilli.

2
00:00:03,318 --> 00:00:05,634
Every spice has its own little bowl.

3
00:00:06,034 --> 00:00:08,521
Seeds go together, powders go together.

4
00:00:08,521 --> 00:00:10,878
Sorting a spice box takes one minute.

5
00:00:11,328 --> 00:00:16,027
Diya sorted her kitchen spice box, then
looked out at the garden and wondered:

6
00:00:16,027 --> 00:00:17,581
who sorts all of this?

7
00:00:17,931 --> 00:00:22,165
Millions of kinds of living things,
and every one needs a place and a name.

8
00:00:22,665 --> 00:00:26,995
By the end, you will know the four
jobs of taxonomy, and what systematics

9
00:00:26,995 --> 00:00:27,982
adds to them.

10
00:00:28,632 --> 00:00:30,153
So what is taxonomy?

11
00:00:30,503 --> 00:00:35,325
Taxonomy is the science of describing,
identifying, grouping and naming

12
00:00:35,325 --> 00:00:36,225
organisms.

13
00:00:36,663 --> 00:00:41,772
It has four basic processes: characterisation,
identification, classification

14
00:00:41,772 --> 00:00:43,104
and nomenclature.

15
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:45,418
Let's take them one at a time.

16
00:00:46,068 --> 00:00:50,352
Taxonomists look at the outer and
inner structure, the structure of the

17
00:00:50,352 --> 00:00:54,385
cells, how the organism develops,
and where and how it lives.

18
00:00:55,035 --> 00:00:59,342
Let's run all four on one plant,
the China rose from Diya's garden.

19
00:00:59,692 --> 00:01:01,720
Process one, characterisation.

20
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:05,667
We describe it part by part:
root, stem, leaf, flower.

21
00:01:06,167 --> 00:01:07,728
Zoom into the flower.

22
00:01:07,728 --> 00:01:09,077
Five large petals.

23
00:01:09,077 --> 00:01:12,676
The stamens are joined into
one tube around the style.

24
00:01:13,176 --> 00:01:14,406
Zoom into a leaf.

25
00:01:14,406 --> 00:01:17,333
A broad, toothed blade,
with a net of veins.

26
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:20,396
Every feature goes into the description.

27
00:01:20,396 --> 00:01:22,812
Now the plant is fully characterised.

28
00:01:23,462 --> 00:01:25,686
Process two, identification.

29
00:01:26,036 --> 00:01:30,374
We compare our description with plants
already known, until one matches.

30
00:01:30,374 --> 00:01:32,942
Now we know exactly which plant this is.

31
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:35,228
Process three, classification.

32
00:01:35,228 --> 00:01:39,225
The plant takes its place in a
group: here, the flowering plants.

33
00:01:39,675 --> 00:01:42,908
Process four, nomenclature,
which means naming.

34
00:01:42,908 --> 00:01:46,418
It gets its scientific name:
Hibiscus rosa sinensis.

35
00:01:47,068 --> 00:01:48,818
Why does this order matter?

36
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:50,200
One step at a time.

37
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:51,450
Step one.

38
00:01:51,404 --> 00:01:55,947
A name only means something once
the organism is described correctly.

39
00:01:56,697 --> 00:01:57,597
Step two.

40
00:01:57,514 --> 00:02:00,216
So identification must come before naming.

41
00:02:00,216 --> 00:02:03,146
You must know what the
name is attached to.

42
00:02:03,896 --> 00:02:04,826
Step three.

43
00:02:04,826 --> 00:02:09,244
Classification places it with its
relatives, so one name points to one

44
00:02:09,244 --> 00:02:10,144
group.

45
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:11,517
Step four.

46
00:02:11,517 --> 00:02:16,600
Long ago, humans sorted living things
only by their use, for food, clothing

47
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:17,610
and shelter.

48
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,006
Taxonomy sorts by features instead.

49
00:02:20,756 --> 00:02:22,113
Now, systematics.

50
00:02:22,463 --> 00:02:27,227
The word comes from the Latin word systema,
which means a systematic arrangement.

51
00:02:27,677 --> 00:02:32,346
Carolus Linnaeus used it in the title
of his book, Systema Naturae, first

52
00:02:32,346 --> 00:02:34,541
printed in seventeen thirty five.

53
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:38,967
Systematics covers identification,
naming and classification.

54
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:44,180
And it adds one more question: how are
these organisms related by evolution?

55
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:46,600
Put the two side by side.

56
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:50,976
Taxonomy asks: what is it, which group
does it belong to, and what do we

57
00:02:50,976 --> 00:02:51,876
call it?

58
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:56,500
Systematics also asks: which groups
share a common ancestor, and how did

59
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:57,791
they branch apart?

60
00:02:58,391 --> 00:02:59,302
Your turn.

61
00:02:59,302 --> 00:03:03,982
A scientist draws a tree showing
which groups share a common ancestor.

62
00:03:03,982 --> 00:03:05,845
Taxonomy, or systematics?

63
00:03:05,845 --> 00:03:07,185
Pause and decide.

64
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:11,433
Systematics!

65
00:03:11,433 --> 00:03:15,187
Taxonomy describes,
identifies, groups and names.

66
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:18,813
Systematics also studies
evolutionary relationships.

67
00:03:19,463 --> 00:03:20,643
Remember this.

68
00:03:20,643 --> 00:03:23,784
Describe, identify, classify, then name.

69
00:03:23,784 --> 00:03:25,747
Systematics adds evolution.

70
00:03:26,347 --> 00:03:29,216
Why does any of this matter
outside the classroom?

71
00:03:29,566 --> 00:03:32,817
Farmers need to know a pest
from a harmless insect.

72
00:03:32,817 --> 00:03:36,059
Forest officers need to
know which trees grow where.

73
00:03:36,509 --> 00:03:40,312
Industries need the right plant
for a medicine or a fibre.

74
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:43,286
Taxonomy is how we know
our living resources.

75
00:03:43,936 --> 00:03:45,618
Now, a worked example.

76
00:03:45,618 --> 00:03:48,135
A new insect turns up in the garden.

77
00:03:48,135 --> 00:03:50,025
Name each process in order.

78
00:03:50,375 --> 00:03:54,412
Diya notes six jointed legs,
two wings and large eyes.

79
00:03:54,412 --> 00:03:55,695
Characterisation.

80
00:03:56,145 --> 00:04:00,552
She compares it with a guide to
known insects, and finds the match.

81
00:04:00,552 --> 00:04:01,595
Identification.

82
00:04:02,045 --> 00:04:04,448
She places it in the insect group.

83
00:04:04,448 --> 00:04:05,522
Classification.

84
00:04:05,972 --> 00:04:09,232
Only then does she write
down its scientific name.

85
00:04:09,232 --> 00:04:10,639
Nomenclature, last.

86
00:04:11,289 --> 00:04:12,754
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:13,104 --> 00:04:17,669
Many students think we name an
organism first, and identify it later.

88
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:21,921
But a name only works when we
know what it is attached to.

89
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:24,411
Identification comes before nomenclature.

90
00:04:25,061 --> 00:04:26,573
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:26,573 --> 00:04:31,403
As statement questions: the basic
processes of taxonomy, and what systematics

92
00:04:31,403 --> 00:04:32,303
adds.

93
00:04:32,572 --> 00:04:33,790
Exam level now.

94
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:35,586
Read the four statements.

95
00:04:35,586 --> 00:04:37,279
Which ones are correct?

96
00:04:37,279 --> 00:04:38,549
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:43,949 --> 00:04:45,238
The answer is D.

98
00:04:45,238 --> 00:04:47,338
Statements one, two and four.

99
00:04:47,838 --> 00:04:49,073
Three is wrong.

100
00:04:49,073 --> 00:04:52,746
Systematics takes evolutionary
relationships into account.

101
00:04:53,246 --> 00:04:55,264
One lists the four processes.

102
00:04:55,264 --> 00:04:57,394
Two is the meaning of systema.

103
00:04:57,394 --> 00:04:59,635
Four is the order of the steps.

104
00:05:00,234 --> 00:05:01,697
Here is your recap.

105
00:05:01,697 --> 00:05:03,201
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:06,401 --> 00:05:09,237
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:05:09,637 --> 00:05:10,537
One.

108
00:05:10,198 --> 00:05:13,529
What are the four basic
processes of taxonomy?

109
00:05:14,429 --> 00:05:15,329
Two.

110
00:05:14,997 --> 00:05:18,071
Why must identification
come before naming?

111
00:05:18,971 --> 00:05:19,871
Three.

112
00:05:19,628 --> 00:05:22,328
What does systematics add to taxonomy?

113
00:05:23,428 --> 00:05:27,512
Next time: a postal address
finds one house among millions.

114
00:05:27,512 --> 00:05:29,931
Living things have an address too.

115
00:05:29,931 --> 00:05:33,313
We find out in taxonomic
categories and hierarchy.
